## Don't use the setTimeout recipe

...

This is the pattern you'll find everywhere, including in the first version of this post:

...

```
const controller = new AbortController();
setTimeout(() => controller.abort(), 10000);
const res = await fetch(url, { signal: controller.signal });
```

...

It does abort slow requests. But nothing ever clears the timer, so in Node.js it keeps the process alive until the timer fires, even after the request has finished. A script that fetches one fast URL and should exit at once:

...

$ node exit-new.mjs        # AbortSignal.timeout(10000)
AbortSignal.timeout: got {"ok":true} at 33ms
AbortSignal.timeout: process exited after 33ms
```

...

In a CLI or a test suite that's ten seconds of hanging for nothing. On a server it's one live timer per request, each holding its `AbortController` until it fires. It also reports a timeout as an `AbortError`, so you can't tell a timeout from any other cancel. `AbortSignal.timeout()` fixes both problems. If you really must use `setTimeout`, clear it in a `finally` block.

...

## What the timeout covers

...

The test server has two slow endpoints. `/slow` waits 3 seconds before sending anything. `/drip` sends its headers right away and then one byte every 500 ms for 10 seconds. That second case is important: `await fetch()` resolves as soon as the headers arrive, and the body is only read when you call `res.json()` or `res.text()`.

...

The signal stays attached to the response, so the deadline covers the whole exchange: connecting, waiting for headers, and reading the body. A timeout while you're reading the body rejects the `res.text()` promise with the same `TimeoutError`.

...

That matters more than it sounds. Node's fetch (undici) has built-in timeouts of its own: 10 s to connect, 300 s to receive headers, and 300 s of silence between body chunks. A response that keeps trickling in never trips any of them. The drip endpoint with no signal at all just runs to the end:

...

A server that sends a byte every few minutes would hold your request open indefinitely. Only a total deadline that you set yourself prevents that.

...

## A small helper: timeout plus your own signal

...

Usually you want both: a deadline, and a way to cancel early, for example when the user leaves the page or clicks Cancel. `AbortSignal.any()` combines signals into one that fires when the first of them fires:

...

`fetch-with-timeout.mjs`:

...

```
/**
 * fetch() with a total deadline that covers connecting, waiting for headers
 * and reading the body. Pass your own `signal` too, to cancel it early.
 */
export function fetchWithTimeout(url, { timeout = 10_000, signal, ...init } = {}) {
  const deadline = AbortSignal.timeout(timeout);
  return fetch(url, {
    ...init,
    signal: signal ? AbortSignal.any([signal, deadline]) : deadline,
  });
}
```

...

Since the signal that fired becomes the rejection reason, `err.name` tells you what happened:

...

- `TimeoutError`: the deadline passed.
- `AbortError`: your own signal was aborted, i.e. the user cancelled.
- Anything else: the request failed at the network level. In Node that's a `TypeError: fetch failed`, with the real reason (`ECONNREFUSED`, `ENOTFOUND`, …) in `err.cause`.

...

The rejection is the signal's `reason` object itself, a `DOMException`. A test against both Node versions confirmed `err === signal.reason`.

...

async function getJson(url, options) {
  const started = performance.now();
  const took = () => `${Math.round(performance.now() - started)}ms`;
  try {
    const res = await fetchWithTimeout(url, options);
    if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${res.status}`);
    // The deadline still applies while the body is being read.
    const body = await res.text();
    return `ok after ${took()}: ${body}`;
  } catch (err) {
    if (err.name === 'TimeoutError') return `timed out after ${took()}`;
    if (err.name === 'AbortError') return `cancelled after ${took()}`;
    // Anything else is a network-level failure: DNS, refused connection, TLS…
    return `failed after ${took()}: ${err.message} (${err.cause?.code})`;
  }
}

